# Shell scripts run on Linux — a CRLF shebang is "bad interpreter", and the one in
# .github/scripts is executed by a GitHub runner on every release.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Compared byte for byte by notificationSites.test.mjs against what
# scripts/count-notifications.mjs prints, and the script writes LF. Without this, git checks it
# out with CRLF on Windows and the drift test goes red on a file nobody has touched - which it
# did, on a rebase, hiding what that test is actually for.
src_vs_code/notification-sites.json text eol=lf

# The same shape, one story later: TheRefusalRoadsAreCountedTests writes this with
# COAI_RECORD_REFUSAL_SITES=1 and JsonSerializer emits LF. Its comparison is a deserialisation, so
# CRLF does not turn it red - it just leaves `git status` claiming a change on a file nobody edited,
# which is how a whitespace-only diff ends up in somebody's commit.
shared/refusal-sites.json text eol=lf

# C# source, because `dotnet format --verify-no-changes` reads `.editorconfig`, which says
# `end_of_line = lf` — measured, since the files on disk carry LF and CI runs on ubuntu. Exactly one
# `.cs` file had ever been committed with CRLF, and it turned the formatting gate red for everybody
# who opened a pull request afterwards, over a file they had not touched. Without this line the
# next one authored on Windows does the same.
*.cs text eol=lf

# The gate's shipped command texts (issue #467). Single lines read by both halves; LF so a Windows
# checkout cannot put a carriage return into an order.
shared/commands/*.md text eol=lf

# The outliner's fixtures and their goldens (S1.3): real source in seven languages whose line numbers
# are part of the expected answer. LF everywhere, so a Windows checkout parses the same bytes CI does.
src_mcp/tests/fixtures/outline/** text eol=lf
# Claude Code writes LF; the caller-transcript fixtures are read by E3 as Claude Code wrote them (E1.S5). core.autocrlf=true would give a .jsonl CRLF.
src_mcp/tests/fixtures/caller-transcripts/** text eol=lf

